"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][5]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M5-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M5-L1 – Data cleaning, missing values and outliers<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Exploratory data analysis and visualisation (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish errors, missing\nobservations and plausible extreme values, and plan traceable cleaning\nsteps.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From raw data to analysis data<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001542_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Sechs Rohwerte, zwei fehlende oder ungültige und vier gültige, darunter ein auffälliger Wert von 75 Sekunden.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Sechs Rohwerte, zwei fehlende oder\nungültige und vier gültige, darunter ein auffälliger Wert von 75\nSekunden.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Valid, missing and unusual processing times.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A measurement log contains six processing times in seconds: 18, 21,\nmissing, 19, 75 and −3. The last value is impossible for a duration; 75\ns, on the other hand, is unusual but possible. Cleaning begins with a\ndata contract: expected types, units, permissible ranges, keys and the\nmeaning of missing entries. Raw data remain unchanged; a derived table\ncontains the corrected values and flags for each change. A duplicate key\nmust first be investigated: it may indicate a transmission error or two\ngenuinely distinct measurements.<\/p>\n<p>An empty entry is not zero. If −3 is marked invalid, four observed\nvalues remain. The observed completeness of the column is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>66.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4/6=66.7\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic preview shows this status before any possible\nimputation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why values are missing<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen a measurement is present, and otherwise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nData may be missing completely at random (MCAR: missingness depends on\nneither observed nor unobserved values). Under MAR, the probability of\nmissingness may depend on observed quantities but no longer on the\nmissing value itself once those quantities are taken into account. MNAR\nrefers to situations in which this condition does not hold, for example\nwhen particularly long processing times are not recorded. These\nmechanisms cannot usually be determined unambiguously from the available\ndata alone.<\/p>\n<p>Removing incomplete rows can affect groups to different degrees.\nSimple median imputation produces a complete column, but often reduces\nobserved variability and does not reflect uncertainty about the imputed\nvalues. For model training, imputation rules must be estimated\nexclusively from the training data. In exploratory analysis, it is often\nmore useful to report valid observations and missingness rates\ntogether.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnosing outliers<\/h2>\n<p>An unusual value is neither automatically erroneous nor automatically\nan interesting discovery. Check sensor limits, timestamps, units and\nrepeated measurements. For a plausible extreme value, robust measures\n(<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a>)\nand sensitivity analysis are useful: the mean of the four valid\nprocessing times is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>21<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>19<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>75<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>33.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(18+21+19+75)/4=33.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns; without 75 s it would be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>58<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>19.33<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">58/3\\approx19.33<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. The large change shows influence but does not justify deletion. The\ntreatment rule must be substantively justified and documented.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A log contains 100 rows. Eight durations are originally missing and\ntwo others are negative. What is the valid proportion if these cases are\ndistinct? How should the analysis proceed?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: 90 out of 100 durations are valid. Report the reasons for\nthe ten missing or invalid values separately, examine their distribution\nby device and time period, and initially analyse the 90 valid values\nwithout arbitrary imputation. A missing time value does not mean that no\nevent took place.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://r4ds.hadley.nz/EDA\">Wickham, Çetinkaya-Rundel and\nGrolemund: R for Data Science (2e), Chapter 10: Exploratory data\nanalysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/missing_data.html\">pandas:\nWorking with missing data (User Guide)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M5-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M5-L2 – Distributions, quantiles and robust statistics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Exploratory data analysis and visualisation (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L4<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Describe empirical\ndistributions, calculate quantiles and distinguish robust statistics\nfrom those sensitive to outliers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Empirical distribution<\/h2>\n<p>A list of observed numbers can vary simultaneously in location,\nspread and shape. The empirical distribution function of a sample\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1,\\ldots,x_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat F_n(t)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{1}\\{x_i\\leq t\\}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The indicator counts each value that is at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the sorted processing times 10, 12, 13, 15, 50 s,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mn>5<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat F_5(13)=3/5=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe empirical function jumps at every observed value; tied values\nproduce a larger jump. It describes the available data rather than\nproviding exact knowledge of the underlying distribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Quantiles and robust location<\/h2>\n<p>A quantile separates sorted observations according to rank. For this\nexample, we use linear interpolation with index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=(n-1)p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\leq p\\leq1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand zero-based indices. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfalls between two indices, interpolate according to its fractional part.\nThis is not the only convention; the method selected in software must\ntherefore be documented.<\/p>\n<p>For five values,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_1=x_{(2)}=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_2=x_{(3)}=13<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_3=x_{(4)}=15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns (here ranks start at one). The interquartile range is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">IQR=Q_3-Q_1=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. The median absolute deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">median<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">median<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MAD=\\operatorname{median}(|x_i-\\operatorname{median}(x)|)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be used as a robust measure of spread. The deviations are 3, 1, 0,\n2, 37; their median is 2 s. The unscaled MAD is not the same as the\nstandard deviation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sensitivity of statistics<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001693_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Boxplot mit Quartilen 12 und 15, Median 13, oberer Tukey-Grenze 19,5 und separat dargestelltem Wert 50.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Boxplot mit Quartilen 12 und 15, Median\n13, oberer Tukey-Grenze 19,5 und separat dargestelltem Wert\n50.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Robust statistics in a box plot.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The arithmetic mean is 20 s. It exceeds the median because the\nlargest value has a strong influence. If only 50 is replaced by 100, the\nmean increases to 30 s, while the median, IQR and MAD remain unchanged.\nRobust measures are nevertheless not immune to arbitrarily many\nerroneous observations. A box plot shows the median, quartiles and,\naccording to a specified convention, outer values; the Tukey fences in\nthe example are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12-1.5\\cdot3=7.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>19.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15+1.5\\cdot3=19.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. The whiskers end at 10 and 15 s, and 50 s is displayed separately.\nThe fences are a diagnostic aid, not proof of errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Replace 50 in the example with 20. Calculate the mean, median, IQR\nand MAD. Which values would the Tukey rule flag as unusual?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: mean 14 s, median 13 s, IQR 3 s and MAD 2 s. The fences\nremain 7.5 and 19.5 s. The new maximum of 20 would therefore also be\nshown separately. The box plot does not assess the substantive\nsituation, only the distance from the middle half of the sample.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://r4ds.hadley.nz/EDA\">Wickham, Çetinkaya-Rundel and\nGrolemund: R for Data Science (2e), Chapter 10: Exploratory data\nanalysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section1/eda15.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: The Role of Graphics, Section 1.1.5<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M5-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M5-L3 – Relationships, correlation and confounding<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Exploratory data analysis and visualisation (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L4<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Describe linear and monotonic\nrelationships and recognise the effect of pooling groups.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Considering two variables together<\/h2>\n<p>For paired observations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x_i,y_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na scatter plot addresses whether certain values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntend to occur with certain values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen both quantities have positive variability, the Pearson correlation\ncoefficient is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msqrt><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=\\frac{\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)(y_i-\\bar y)}{\\sqrt{\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)^2\\sum_i(y_i-\\bar y)^2}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It lies between −1 and 1 and measures linear association. Shifting\nall values or scaling them by a positive factor does not change it. If a\nvariable is constant,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis undefined. A strong curved relationship may nevertheless have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith symmetric\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvalues. Spearman correlation is the Pearson correlation of the ranks\n(using average ranks for ties); it can capture monotonic, nonlinear\nrelationships more effectively, but does not detect every\ndependence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When groups reverse the sign<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001613_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zwei abfallende Punktgruppen mit jeweils r minus eins; gepoolte Gerade steigt, r etwa 0,722.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zwei abfallende Punktgruppen mit jeweils\nr minus eins; gepoolte Gerade steigt, r etwa 0,722.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Pooling groups changes correlation.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A synthetic experiment observes processing load\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand duration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin two system classes. In class A, the pairs are (1,9), (2,8), (3,7); in\nclass B, (4,15), (5,14), (6,13). Within both classes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculated across both classes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.722<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\approx0.722<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe difference between the classes dominates the negative relationship\nwithin each class. The graphic preview shows both groups and the pooled\nline. This reversal of direction is an example of Simpson’s paradox in\nthe broad sense.<\/p>\n<p>If system class influences both variables, it is a potential\nconfounder. Stratification can then reveal different relationships.\nWhether a variable should actually be controlled for depends on the\ncausal structure; conversely, controlling for a common effect of two\nvariables can induce a spurious correlation. The graphic alone\nestablishes neither a cause nor the correct adjustment strategy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Robust analysis and interpretation<\/h2>\n<p>Examine the scatter plot first, then calculate statistics. A single\ndistant point can substantially change Pearson correlation. With\nrepeated observations of a device or temporally dependent data, the\npoints must not simply be treated as independent units for subsequent\ninference. Correlation is also symmetric:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(x,y)=r(y,x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhereas predicting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a directional task.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>The values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(-2,-1,0,1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=(4,1,0,1,4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncome from an error-free quadratic relationship. Why is Pearson\ncorrelation nevertheless zero?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar y=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the terms\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i(y_i-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare −4, 1, 0, −1 and 4. Their sum is zero, and both variables have\npositive variability. The result rules out linear association in this\nsample, not the deterministic relationship.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://r4ds.hadley.nz/EDA\">Wickham, Çetinkaya-Rundel and\nGrolemund: R for Data Science (2e), Chapter 10: Exploratory data\nanalysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://clauswilke.com/dataviz/directory-of-visualizations.html\">Wilke:\nFundamentals of Data Visualization, Chapter 5: Directory of\nvisualizations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M5-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M5-L4 – Visualisation of numerical and categorical data<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Exploratory data analysis and visualisation (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Select chart types appropriate\nto the variables and question, and interpret their scales correctly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measured quantities and visual mapping<\/h2>\n<p>A graphic maps data values to visual attributes: position, length,\ncolour, shape or area. The choice begins with the question. Bars are\nsuitable for frequencies of nominal categories; a histogram or empirical\ndistribution function is suitable for a numerical distribution. An\nordered time axis may be represented by a line. Connecting two unordered\ncategories, by contrast, would suggest an intermediate progression that\ndoes not exist. Bar lengths require a zero baseline; for a dot or line\nchart, a justified and clearly labelled restricted range may be\nuseful.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scaling histograms correctly<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001593_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Drei Histogrammklassen mit Breiten 5, 5 und 10 Sekunden und Dichten 0,1, 0,075 und 0,0125 pro Sekunde.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Drei Histogrammklassen mit Breiten 5, 5\nund 10 Sekunden und Dichten 0,1, 0,075 und 0,0125 pro\nSekunde.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Histogram with unequal bin widths.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>With bin boundaries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_j&lt;b_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand counts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe normalised histogram height is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_j=\\frac{c_j}{n(b_j-a_j)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The area of a bar therefore corresponds to its relative frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_j/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nExample: eight processing times of 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12 s are divided\ninto [0,5), [5,10) and [10,20). The counts are 4, 3, 1; the densities\nare 0.1, 0.075 and 0.0125 per second. All areas sum to one. The graphic\npreview uses these unequal bin widths. If raw counts were used as\nheights instead, the area of the wide final bin would be\ndisproportionately large. The choice of bin width affects which\nstructures are visible; alternative widths are therefore useful.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Categories and comparisons<\/h2>\n<p>For 40 operations in the three categories A, B and C with counts of\n18, 14 and 8, the proportions are 45%, 35% and 20%. A bar chart shows\ntheir magnitudes on a common scale. When comparing two groups of\ndifferent sizes, proportions normalised within each group are often more\ninformative than absolute counts. Both quantities matter: proportions\nshow composition, counts show size. Ordinal categories retain their\nmeaningful order, even if the frequency order differs. Labels should\nspecify units, the population or sample size, and any missing cases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A bin [20,30) contains 5 of a total of 50 measurements. What is the\nheight of the density bar? How high would a bar with the same count and\ntwice the width be?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5/(50\\cdot10)=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper unit. With width 20, the height is 0.005 per unit; the area is 0.1\nin both cases. A bar chart of nominal categories has no such numerical\nbin widths.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://clauswilke.com/dataviz/directory-of-visualizations.html\">Wilke:\nFundamentals of Data Visualization, Chapter 5: Directory of\nvisualizations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section1/eda15.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: The Role of Graphics, Section 1.1.5<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M5-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M5-L5 – Multidimensional data and interactive exploration<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Exploratory data analysis and visualisation (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explore multiple variables\ntogether using small multiples, colour and shape encoding, and\nconsistent filters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>More than two features<\/h2>\n<p>An observation can have numerous variables, such as temperature,\nload, processing time and device type. A two-dimensional representation\ncan encode load and processing time as coordinates, and type as shape or\ncolour. Further features can be represented using separate panels\n(facets) rather than ever more visual attributes. Facets with shared\naxes support comparisons between groups; differing axes must be clearly\nindicated. With many data points, symbols overlap: transparency,\ntwo-dimensional binning or density plots are alternatives.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The correlation matrix and its limitations<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnumerical variables, a full matrix contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nentries, including\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(d-1)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndistinct pairs. A correlation matrix has ones on the diagonal provided\nthe respective variable is not constant. A diverging colour scale from\n−1 to +1 with a neutral midpoint at zero keeps signs and strengths\ncomparable. Calculation using pairwise exclusion of missing data may use\na different sample for each pair; sample sizes must then be reported.\nFurthermore, a matrix estimated this way can lose its positive\nsemidefiniteness. A correlation table cannot replace scatter plots:\nnonlinear and group-specific relationships may remain hidden.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Filtering, linking and comparing<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001731_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Drei ausgewählte Punkte in einem Streudiagramm; die rechte Ansicht zeigt ihren Mittelwert 14 s im Vergleich zu 11 s für alle sechs Punkte.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Drei ausgewählte Punkte in einem\nStreudiagramm; die rechte Ansicht zeigt ihren Mittelwert 14 s im\nVergleich zu 11 s für alle sechs Punkte.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Linked selection in two views.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>An interactive dashboard should make selection states visible.\nSuppose six operations have loads (1,2,3,4,5,6) and durations\n(9,8,7,15,14,13) as in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3<\/a>.\nThe filter for load at least 4 highlights three observations. Their mean\nis 14 s compared with 11 s in the entire sample. A linked second view\nshould highlight the same three IDs rather than silently using a\ndifferent population. The graphic preview shows the selection in the\nscatter plot and the mean-value view. The means answer different\nquestions; their difference is not evidence of an intervention\neffect.<\/p>\n<p>With an interactive time selection, time boundaries and the time zone\nmust remain unambiguous. Filters and aggregation steps should be\nexportable. For accessible presentation, a combination of shape, text\nand colour identifies the selection so that it can also be recognised\nwithout colour perception.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>How many distinct variable pairs does a matrix of five features\ncontain? How can the selection of operations with loads from 2 to 4 be\ndocumented reproducibly across two charts?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\cdot4/2=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npairs. Save the condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\leq x\\leq4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe dataset version and the associated observation IDs; this produces\nthree points with durations of 8, 7 and 15 s. Their mean is 10 s. The\nselected case count must be the same in both views, provided no further\nexclusions are applied.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://clauswilke.com/dataviz/directory-of-visualizations.html\">Wilke:\nFundamentals of Data Visualization, Chapter 5: Directory of\nvisualizations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://r4ds.hadley.nz/EDA\">Wickham, Çetinkaya-Rundel and\nGrolemund: R for Data Science (2e), Chapter 10: Exploratory data\nanalysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M5-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M5-L6 – Sampling error, misleading graphics and communication<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Exploratory data analysis and visualisation (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L7<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish sampling\nvariability, systematic bias and visual exaggeration, and communicate\nresults transparently.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Variability and systematic errors<\/h2>\n<p>Sampling error in the narrower statistical sense is the random\ndeviation of an estimate from its target value due to sample selection.\nFor independent, identically distributed Bernoulli observations with\nsuccess probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe proportion estimator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{p(1-p)/n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstituting the observed proportion for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields an estimated standard error. With 54 successes in 100\nobservations,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.54<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat p=0.54<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0498<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat{SE}\\approx0.0498<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is nearly five percentage points, not five per cent relative to the\nestimated proportion. Standard errors and confidence intervals are\ndifferent (M6).<\/p>\n<p>A voluntary survey can have a biased composition even with very large\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMore responses reduce random variation under suitable assumptions, but\ndo not automatically remedy selection, measurement or non-response bias.\nMissing groups must therefore be investigated before interpretation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Displays with deceptive scales<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001504_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zwei Balkendiagramme derselben Anteile, links mit Nullbasis, rechts bei 94 Prozent abgeschnitten und als Negativbeispiel markiert.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zwei Balkendiagramme derselben Anteile,\nlinks mit Nullbasis, rechts bei 94 Prozent abgeschnitten und als\nNegativbeispiel markiert.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Same data, different axis effect.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Two hypothetical systems achieve values of 96% and 98%. The\ndifference is 2 percentage points; relative to the first value, it is\napproximately 2.083%. If a bar chart starts at 94%, the visible bar\nlengths are 2 and 4 percentage points respectively. This makes the\nsecond bar appear twice as long, although the proportion is only\nslightly greater. The graphic preview contrasts the truncated chart as a\nnegative example with a zero baseline. For a detailed comparison, a dot\nplot with an explicitly restricted scale may be more suitable.<\/p>\n<p>Unequal time intervals drawn on an equally spaced axis, undisclosed\nsmoothing or a change of denominator can also create a discrepancy\nbetween the impression and the data. If uncertainty bars are shown,\ntheir meaning (standard deviation, standard error, interval or something\nelse) must be explained immediately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A transparent results report<\/h2>\n<p>First state which population and time period the analysis covers.\nDistinguish observed findings, uncertainty and your own interpretation.\nSpecify which data were excluded, how units are defined and how\nstatistics were calculated. An exploratory finding can motivate a new\nhypothesis; a confirmatory investigation then requires appropriate,\npreferably independent data, or an analysis explicitly labelled as\nexploratory. Data protection and access rules may limit publication of\nraw data, but not the need for a transparent description of methods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A proportion increases from 50% to 55% in two independent samples of\n100 cases each. Calculate the estimated standard error of the difference\nunder independent Bernoulli sampling. What follows?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.55<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.45<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0705<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{0.5\\cdot0.5/100+0.55\\cdot0.45/100}\\approx0.0705<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe observed difference of five percentage points is smaller than this\nestimated standard error. A definite increase cannot be inferred from\nthis; a formal test follows in M6.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://clauswilke.com/dataviz/directory-of-visualizations.html\">Wilke:\nFundamentals of Data Visualization, Chapter 5: Directory of\nvisualizations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section1/eda15.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: The Role of Graphics, Section 1.1.5<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M5-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M5-L7 – Exploratory case study with an original dataset<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Exploratory data analysis and visualisation (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Carry out a complete exploratory\nworkflow with documented data checks, group comparisons and\nappropriately limited conclusions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research question and synthetic data<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional engineering team investigates processing times of two\ndevices handling orders logged in the same way. The eight records were\nconstructed specifically for this exercise; they do not come from real\nmeasurements. Each row is an order; <code>last<\/code> is a dimensionless\nload level, and <code>dauer_s<\/code> is the duration in seconds. The\nproject question is: what differences appear between devices and load\nlevels, and where are data missing?<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>ID<\/th>\n<th>Device<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Load<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Duration (s)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">14<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">–<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>ID must be unique, and load and duration must be positive where\nobserved. The missing duration remains unknown; 30 s is unusual, but not\nautomatically invalid.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Analysis with Python<\/h2>\n<p>The following program reconstructs the data in full. It requires only\npandas and calculates group sizes, valid cases and measures of location.\n<code>size<\/code> also counts rows without a duration, whereas\n<code>count<\/code> counts only available durations.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> pandas <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> pd<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>df <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> pd.DataFrame({<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"st\">&quot;id&quot;<\/span>: <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"st\">&quot;geraet&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"st\">&quot;last&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"st\">&quot;dauer_s&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">14<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>})<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> df[<span class=\"st\">&quot;id&quot;<\/span>].is_unique<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> df[<span class=\"st\">&quot;last&quot;<\/span>].gt(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>).<span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> df[<span class=\"st\">&quot;dauer_s&quot;<\/span>].dropna().gt(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>).<span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>bericht <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> df.groupby(<span class=\"st\">&quot;geraet&quot;<\/span>)[<span class=\"st\">&quot;dauer_s&quot;<\/span>].agg(<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    zeilen<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;size&quot;<\/span>, gueltig<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;count&quot;<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    mittel<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;mean&quot;<\/span>, median<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;median&quot;<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>bericht[<span class=\"st\">&quot;fehlquote&quot;<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> bericht.gueltig <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> bericht.zeilen<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(bericht)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>paare <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> df.pivot(index<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;last&quot;<\/span>, columns<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;geraet&quot;<\/span>, values<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;dauer_s&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>differenzen <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (paare[<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> paare[<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>]).dropna()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(differenzen.mean())<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Device A: three valid values, mean 12 s, median 12 s, missingness\nrate 25%. Device B: four valid values, mean 15 s, median 11 s,\nmissingness rate 0%. The unpaired mean appears to favour A. At the three\nshared load levels, however, B is 2 s faster each time; the mean paired\ndifference A minus B is 2 s.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Graphical analysis and limitations<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001572_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zwei Laufzeitkurven; B liegt bei Last 1 bis 3 zwei Sekunden unter A, bei Last 4 erreicht B 30 Sekunden, während A fehlt.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zwei Laufzeitkurven; B liegt bei Last 1\nbis 3 zwei Sekunden unter A, bei Last 4 erreicht B 30 Sekunden, während\nA fehlt.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Device processing times by load level.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The graphic preview connects only consecutive observed load levels\nfor each device. A has a visibly missing observation at load 4, while B\nincreases to 30 s. Shared load level 4 must not be interpreted as an\nequivalently observed comparison. Whether one device is generally faster\nremains open: there are no repetitions, independent orders or\nexplanations for the missing measurement. The three differences describe\nonly the constructed dataset. For the next data collection, both devices\nshould be measured repeatedly under identical load conditions, with\ncauses of failures recorded.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate the overall mean of the valid durations. As a sensitivity\nanalysis, repeat the calculation without the unusual value of 30 s. What\nmay be concluded?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>96<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>13.71<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(10+12+14+8+10+12+30)/7=96/7\\approx13.71<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. Without 30 s, 66/6=11 s remains. The difference shows the sensitivity\nof the mean, but not that the removed value is erroneous. Report both\nscenarios with case counts and keep the raw data unchanged.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://r4ds.hadley.nz/EDA\">Wickham, Çetinkaya-Rundel and\nGrolemund: R for Data Science (2e), Chapter 10: Exploratory data\nanalysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/missing_data.html\">pandas:\nWorking with missing data (User Guide)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://clauswilke.com/dataviz/directory-of-visualizations.html\">Wilke:\nFundamentals of Data Visualization, Chapter 5: Directory of\nvisualizations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
